            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Consulships</strong><br>\nMarius was elected consul an unprecedented seven times between 107 and 86 BCE, which was a clear sign of his popularity and political influence in Rome. During his terms, he continued to push for reforms that would strengthen Rome's military and his own political position. His repeated consulships also marked a significant deviation from Roman political norms, which typically limited such power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conflict with Sulla</strong><br>\nThe political rivalry between Marius and Lucius Cornelius Sulla defined the late Roman Republic's politics. Sulla was a patrician and represented the aristocratic interests, opposing Marius's populistic approach. Their conflict eventually led to Sulla's march on Rome in 88 BCE, an unprecedented act that signaled the breakdown of the Republic's norms and the onset of civil war.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Social War</strong><br>\nDuring the Social War from 91 to 88 BCE, Marius played a critical role in the conflict between Rome and its Italian allies, who fought for Roman citizenship and equal rights. Although Marius was not the sole military leader in this war, his experience and military reforms were significant in the eventual Roman victory and the integration of Italian allies into the Roman political community.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
