          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nMarius's later years were marked by political turmoil and personal struggle. After being ousted by Sulla, he aligned with the populares faction in Rome, advocating for the rights of the common people. He lived to see his return to power in 86 BCE, during his seventh consulship, but died shortly after at the age of 71, under circumstances that suggested the end of one era and the beginning of another in Roman history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nMarius's legacy is a complex one. He is remembered for his military prowess and reforms, which had a lasting impact on Rome's military structure. However, his political maneuvering contributed to the erosion of the Republic's traditions and stability. His career paved the way for future military leaders to become pivotal in Roman politics, ultimately leading to the end of the Republic and the rise of the Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on the Military and Republic</strong><br>\nMarius's influence extended beyond his lifetime. His military reforms not only professionalized the Roman army but also inadvertently increased generals' power, making armies more loyal to their commanders than the Republic itself. This shift had profound implications for Rome's future, as it set a precedent for military might to dictate political authority, changing the course of Roman history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
